    "abstract": "Climate change became real for many Americans in 2012 when a record heat wave affected much of the United States, and Superstorm Sandy pounded the Northeast. At the same time, a less visible heat wave was occurring over a large portion of the Northwest Atlantic Ocean. Like the heat wave on land, the ocean heat wave affected coastal ecosystems and economies. Marine species responded to warmer temperatures by shifting their geographic distribution and seasonal cycles. Warm-water species moved northward, and some species undertook local migrations earlier in the season, both of which affected fisheries targeting those species. Extreme events are expected to become more common as climate change progresses (Tebaldi et al., 2006; Hansen et al., 2012). The 2012 Northwest Atlantic heat wave provides valuable insights into ways scientific information streams and fishery management frameworks may need to adapt to be effective as ocean temperatures warm and become more variable.",
    "field": "Fisheries management and climate change",
    "problem": "Current fishery management frameworks struggle to adapt to rapid, climate-driven ecosystem changes because they are often based on stationary biological baselines and inflexible governance structures, which prove inadequate during extreme events like the 2012 ocean heat wave.",
    "method": "The authors propose adapting fisheries management to climate change by developing predictive ecosystem models and creating nimble management systems that can adjust permits, boundaries, and biological baselines in response to abrupt, climate-driven ecosystem changes.",
    "task": "The task is to use the 2012 Northwest Atlantic ocean heat wave as a case study to identify how fisheries management must adapt to a warming and more variable climate.",
